---->Compile time  Vs  Runtime
Compile time = building and preparing the model
Runtime = actually executing your simulation and testbench

--dynamic binding (virtual functions) is decided at runtime, not compile time.
That?s why we call it runtime polymorphism.

Ex:
Compile time ? ?Is your code valid and ready to run??
Runtime ? ?Now the code is running ? execute the behavior.?
*/

/*
================================================================================
SystemVerilog Randomization & Constraints Notes
================================================================================

1. Randomization Overview
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
- Randomization allows generating different values for variables at runtime.
- Example:
    class packet;
      rand bit [7:0] addr;
      rand bit [7:0] data;
    endclass
- Use: p.randomize(); generates random values for addr & data.

Doubt clarification:
- Q: "Why do we need randomize() if variables are declared rand/randc?"
- A: Declaring rand/randc only marks the variable as randomizable. 
     randomize() triggers the solver to generate a value **at runtime**, 
     considering constraints, inheritance, and previous values.  


2. rand vs randc
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
- rand: fully random value every time (e.g., rand bit [7:0] a;)
- randc: cyclic random ? cycles through all values before repeating
- Use randc when all possible values should appear once before repeat.


3. randomize() Method
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
- Triggers the SystemVerilog solver to assign values to rand variables.
- Returns 1 if successful, 0 if constraints cannot be satisfied.
- Always check result:
    if (!p.randomize()) $display("Randomization failed!");


4. Constraints
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
- Constraints define rules or limits on random values.
- Examples:
    constraint addr_c { addr < 100; }           // Range constraint
    constraint data_c { data inside {[10:20]}; } // Set membership
- Types:
    1. Range: a > 10 && a < 100
    2. Set membership: b inside {1,3,5,7,9}
    3. Relational: a < b
    4. Distribution: addr dist { [0:50] := 1, [51:100] := 5 }
       (weighted probabilities)


5. Conditional Constraints
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
- Constraints applied only under certain conditions.
- Example:
    constraint c { if (mode==1) addr<50; else addr>200; }


6. Enabling & Disabling Constraints
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
- Each constraint can be turned on/off at runtime:
    p.addr_c.constraint_mode(0); // disable
    p.addr_c.constraint_mode(1); // enable


7. rand_mode()
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
- Disable randomization for a specific variable:
    p.addr.rand_mode(0); // addr stays fixed


8. Inline (Temporary) Constraints
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
- Override constraints for a single randomization call:
    p.randomize() with { addr>200; data<50; };


9. pre_randomize() & post_randomize()
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
- pre_randomize(): called automatically **before randomization**
- post_randomize(): called automatically **after randomization**
- Use cases:
    - Initialize or adjust variables (pre_randomize)
    - Logging or validating values (post_randomize)


10. Inheritance & Randomization
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
- Child classes inherit parent rand variables and constraints.
- Randomization considers all constraints together.
- Example:
    class base; rand int a; constraint c_base { a>0; } endclass
    class child extends base; constraint c_child { a<10; } endclass
- Generated a ? (1,9)


11. Handling Randomization Failures
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
- Conflicting constraints make randomize() fail.
- Always check:
    if (!p.randomize()) $display("Randomization failed!");
- Example:
    constraint c1 { a>100; } constraint c2 { a<50; } ? fails


12. DV Example
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
- Randomized packet generation to avoid manually writing hundreds of test cases.
- Example:
    class packet;
      rand bit [3:0] src,dest;
      rand bit [7:0] payload;
      constraint valid_range { src inside {[1:8]}; dest inside {[1:8]}; src != dest; }
      function void display(); $display("src=%0d dest=%0d payload=%0h", src,dest,payload); endfunction
    endclass

- Output example:
    src=5 dest=3 payload=9a
    src=2 dest=7 payload=6e
    src=8 dest=1 payload=ff


13. Connection to UVM
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
- In UVM, all transactions (sequence_items) are randomizable classes.
- Constraints and randomization logic transfer directly:
    class packet extends uvm_sequence_item;
      rand bit [3:0] addr;
      rand bit [7:0] data;
      constraint c { addr<10; }
      `uvm_object_utils(packet)
    endclass
- Example usage in sequences:
    seq_item.randomize() with { addr==5; };


14. Key Takeaways / DV Best Practices
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
- Use randomization to **automate stimulus generation** and improve coverage.
- Constraints guide the solver to generate **legal and meaningful values**.
- Always check randomize() return value for failure.
- Use pre/post_randomize() for setup and logging.
- rand_mode() and constraint_mode() allow **runtime flexibility**.
- Randomization + constraints + inheritance = powerful DV technique.
- This knowledge directly applies to UVM transactions (sequence_items).

================================================================================
End of Notes
================================================================================
*/

